WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.210
My name is David Dunnington.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.380
I'm a software engineer
at Voltron Data,

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.310
and I'm a developer on the
Apache Arrow project where

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.620
I get to work on cool
things like geospatial data

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.480
and R. One of the
things that came out

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.940
of that is the geoarrow
package, which picked

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.460
my favorite things about
arrow and my favorite things

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.270
about R, which is r-spatial,
and puts them together.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.250
My first favorite thing
about the arrow package

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.080
are parquet files, though.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.030
Parquet files are a
little bit like CSVs

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.150
in that you can read and write
tables, but they're binary,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.880
so they're smaller and they're
faster to read and write

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.960
and they remember
your data type.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.600
So if you write a
date to a parquet file,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.720
you get a date back.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.510
If you write a number
to a parquet file,

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.070
you get a number back. With geoarrow,

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.160
if you write an sf object
to a parquet file,

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.990
you get an sf object back,
which is really useful.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.290
And it turns out that
it's also really fast.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.785
So if you find yourself waiting
for your files to load, try parquet.

00:00:58.785 --> 00:01:01.410
If you find yourself waiting for
your geospatial files to load,

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:06.010
try `geoarrow::read_geoparquet()`.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.440
My second favorite thing about
the arrow package are data

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.830
sets. Data sets let you
take a whole lot of files

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.740
that you create and you can
split them up however you want

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.970
and you can query them
like they're a database.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.330
In this example, I have a whole
bunch of parking infractions

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.760
from the city of
Philadelphia, and I have

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.920
them hosted on an S3 bucket.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.170
They could be on my computer too.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.710
Here, using the arrow package,

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:32.980
I can open them as
a data set and I

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:34.960
can query them using dplyr.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.115
And when I hit collect, arrow
takes care of the details.

00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:39.990
And there's a lot of
details to take care of.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.040
Arrow pulls the data
from a remote data source

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.520
or from your computer,
and arrow runs your query

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.160
in parallel using all the
cores on your computer,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.290
and it splits up the data
into manageable chunks,

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.900
just in case the data set
is bigger than your memory.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.420
A lot of geospatial data is
really big, and a lot of it

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.280
is bigger than
memory. This lets

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.730
you use that workflow
with geospatial data

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:01.850
and it just works.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.190
The final thing is something
that a lot of users

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.450
don't think about,
but it's something

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.867
that I'm really passionate
about as an R spatial

00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:13.210
package maintainer, which
is simplifying possibly

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.580
how to maintain all of the
components that make up

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.550
the R spatial ecosystem and
how they fit together.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.950
There's a lot of components
and they all come together,

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.910
hopefully silently, to make your
experience as an R spatial user

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.440
awesome.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.820
But there's a lot
of interactions

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.160
and there's a lot of people
that spend a lot of time

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.750
managing all of
those interactions

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.440
and maintaining them.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.090
GeoArrow uses a data
structure that we

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:40.170
can pass between all of those,
which means that we can define

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.240
an interaction for
every single component

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.940
rather than every single
pair of components, which

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.740
is a lot less maintenance work.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.480
And it means that R
spatial developers

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.790
like me can spend more
time adding features,

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:57.157
which is fun, and less time
fixing bugs, which is less fun.

00:02:57.157 --> 00:02:59.490
And finally, because the thing
that we're passing around

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:00.580
is an arrow array,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.120
we get all of the arrow
ecosystem at our disposal,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.050
which includes the arrow package,
parquet files and data sets,

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.120
but also the wider
arrow ecosystem, which

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.610
includes bindings
for Python and Rust

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.510
and Julia and a lot
of other languages.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.420
And a lot of other frameworks
that have adopted arrow.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.900
The geoarrow package is still
under development and as

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.450
well as the Apache Arrow
integration that enables this.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:26.450
But if you want to follow along,
you can follow me on Twitter

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.730
or follow me on GitHub
or visit my website

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.040
where I blog about all sorts
of incredibly nerdy things

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.570
involving geospatial,
R, and arrow.

